  "name": "ETHYL GLUCURONIDE (ETG) & ETHYL SULPHATE (ETS) QUANTITATIVE ASSAY",
  "code": "1019",
  "cost": 0,
  "interpretation": "<p data-start=\"254\" data-end=\"495\">The <strong data-start=\"258\" data-end=\"279\">EtG and EtS tests</strong> are highly sensitive assays used to <strong data-start=\"316\" data-end=\"353\">detect recent alcohol consumption</strong> by measuring two direct metabolites of ethanol&mdash;<strong data-start=\"401\" data-end=\"428\">ethyl glucuronide (EtG)</strong> and <strong data-start=\"433\" data-end=\"456\">ethyl sulfate (EtS)</strong>&mdash;in urine, blood, or other body fluids.</p>\n<p data-start=\"497\" data-end=\"532\">These tests are commonly used in:</p>\n<ul data-start=\"533\" data-end=\"762\">\n<li data-start=\"533\" data-end=\"612\">\n<p data-start=\"535\" data-end=\"612\"><strong data-start=\"535\" data-end=\"560\">Abstinence monitoring</strong> (e.g., in addiction recovery, probation programs)</p>\n</li>\n<li data-start=\"613\" data-end=\"647\">\n<p data-start=\"615\" data-end=\"647\"><strong data-start=\"615\" data-end=\"645\">Pre-transplant evaluations</strong></p>\n</li>\n<li data-start=\"648\" data-end=\"696\">\n<p data-start=\"650\" data-end=\"696\"><strong data-start=\"650\" data-end=\"694\">Forensic and occupational health testing</strong></p>\n</li>\n<li data-start=\"697\" data-end=\"762\">\n<p data-start=\"699\" data-end=\"762\"><strong data-start=\"699\" data-end=\"762\">Clinical assessment of suspected alcohol use despite denial</strong></p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p data-start=\"764\" data-end=\"858\">EtG and EtS are detectable <strong data-start=\"791\" data-end=\"827\">long after ethanol is eliminated</strong> from the body&mdash;typically up to:</p>\n<ul data-start=\"859\" data-end=\"1014\">\n<li data-start=\"859\" data-end=\"915\">\n<p data-start=\"861\" data-end=\"915\"><strong data-start=\"861\" data-end=\"868\">EtG</strong>: ~24&ndash;80 hours after moderate to heavy drinking</p>\n</li>\n<li data-start=\"916\" data-end=\"1014\">\n<p data-start=\"918\" data-end=\"1014\"><strong data-start=\"918\" data-end=\"925\">EtS</strong>: ~24&ndash;48 hours after drinking (slightly shorter window but less prone to false positives)</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p data-start=\"1062\" data-end=\"1268\">High levels of EtG and/or EtS in urine indicate <strong data-start=\"1110\" data-end=\"1138\">recent alcohol ingestion</strong>, even if blood ethanol is no longer detectable. The level can correlate loosely with the <strong data-start=\"1228\" data-end=\"1249\">amount and timing</strong> of alcohol intake:</p>\n<ul data-start=\"1270\" data-end=\"1608\">\n<li data-start=\"1270\" data-end=\"1375\">\n<p data-start=\"1272\" data-end=\"1375\"><strong data-start=\"1272\" data-end=\"1291\">EtG &gt; 500 ng/mL</strong> typically indicates <strong data-start=\"1312\" data-end=\"1345\">moderate to heavy alcohol use</strong> within the past 24&ndash;72 hours</p>\n</li>\n<li data-start=\"1376\" data-end=\"1539\">\n<p data-start=\"1378\" data-end=\"1539\"><strong data-start=\"1378\" data-end=\"1395\">EtS detection</strong> alongside EtG supports the finding and helps <strong data-start=\"1441\" data-end=\"1469\">rule out false positives</strong> from incidental alcohol exposure (e.g., hand sanitizers, mouthwash)</p>\n</li>\n<li data-start=\"1540\" data-end=\"1608\">\n<p data-start=\"1542\" data-end=\"1608\">Very high levels may suggest <strong data-start=\"1571\" data-end=\"1589\">binge drinking</strong> or <strong data-start=\"1593\" data-end=\"1608\">chronic use</strong></p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p data-start=\"1610\" data-end=\"1783\"><strong data-start=\"1610\" data-end=\"1619\">Note:</strong> EtG and EtS are <strong data-start=\"1636\" data-end=\"1671\">not used to assess intoxication</strong>, but <strong data-start=\"1677\" data-end=\"1703\">presence or recent use</strong>. Their accuracy can be affected by sample handling, renal function, and timing.</p>",
